[{"role": "system", "content": "You are a complaint-triage agent for a medical device manufacturer's quality team. For each complaint, decide FDA reportability: REPORTABLE_30DAY, NOT_REPORTABLE, or ESCALATE (when expert judgment is needed). You may look up regulatory guidance first by replying with JSON {"action":"lookup","topic":"<topic>"}. Topics: mdr_reportability, serious_injury, malfunction, reportability_pitfalls, not_reportable_examples, escalation. When ready, reply with JSON {"action":"final","decision":"<one allowed decision>","confidence":<0-1>,"needs_expert_review":<true|false>,"reasoning":"<max 2 sentences>"}. Reply with JSON only."}, {"role": "user", "content": "a child at our care home died overnight while hooked up to the pulse oximeter. there's a gap of about 18 and a half minutes where alarms weren't responded to, and staff are saying the alarm didn't actually go off.
Allowed decisions: REPORTABLE_30DAY|NOT_REPORTABLE|ESCALATE"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```json
{"action":"lookup","topic":"reportability_pitfalls"}
```"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Reportability is not removed by: the patient recovering, the user catching the error, no harm occurring this time, a suspected use error, a labeled interferent, an unconfirmed root cause, or the device not yet being returned. Awareness starts the 30-day clock."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```json
{"action":"final","decision":"REPORTABLE_30DAY","confidence":0.92,"needs_expert_review":true,"reasoning":"A patient death in which the device may have caused or contributed, here an alleged alarm failure during an 18.5-minute unresponded gap, is MDR-reportable regardless of unconfirmed root cause, suspected use error, or device not yet returned, and the 30-day clock started at awareness. Escalate now so experts can judge whether remedial action requires a 5-day report, secure the device and alarm/event logs, and check for similar alarm-failure complaints."}
```"}]

[{"role": "system", "content": "You are a complaint-triage agent for a medical device manufacturer's quality team. For each complaint, decide FDA reportability: REPORTABLE_30DAY, NOT_REPORTABLE, or ESCALATE (when expert judgment is needed). You may look up regulatory guidance first by replying with JSON {"action":"lookup","topic":"<topic>"}. Topics: mdr_reportability, serious_injury, malfunction, reportability_pitfalls, not_reportable_examples, escalation. When ready, reply with JSON {"action":"final","decision":"<one allowed decision>","confidence":<0-1>,"needs_expert_review":<true|false>,"reasoning":"<max 2 sentences>"}. Reply with JSON only."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Complaint record: A facility reports that the control dial on a bedside pulse oximeter was damaged. The device was in use on a patient until the patient died. The reporter did not allege that the device caused or contributed to the death.
Allowed decisions: REPORTABLE_30DAY|NOT_REPORTABLE|ESCALATE"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```json
{"action":"lookup","topic":"reportability_pitfalls"}
```"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Reportability is not removed by: the patient recovering, the user catching the error, no harm occurring this time, a suspected use error, a labeled interferent, an unconfirmed root cause, or the device not yet being returned. Awareness starts the 30-day clock."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```json
{"action":"lookup","topic":"escalation"}
```"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Escalate to a qualified expert when key outcome information is pending, causality is genuinely uncertain, the device may have performed as intended, or reasonable experts would disagree."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```json
{"action":"final","decision":"ESCALATE","confidence":0.7,"needs_expert_review":true,"reasoning":"A patient died while a damaged pulse oximeter was in use, and it is unclear whether the damaged dial affected monitoring or alarms. The reporter not alleging causation does not rule out reportability, so an expert should promptly assess whether this is a reportable death or malfunction while the 30-day clock runs from awareness."}
```"}]

[{"role": "system", "content": "You are a complaint-triage agent for a medical device manufacturer's quality team. For each complaint, decide FDA reportability: REPORTABLE_30DAY, NOT_REPORTABLE, or ESCALATE (when expert judgment is needed). You may look up regulatory guidance first by replying with JSON {"action":"lookup","topic":"<topic>"}. Topics: mdr_reportability, serious_injury, malfunction, reportability_pitfalls, not_reportable_examples, escalation. When ready, reply with JSON {"action":"final","decision":"<one allowed decision>","confidence":<0-1>,"needs_expert_review":<true|false>,"reasoning":"<max 2 sentences>"}. Reply with JSON only."}, {"role": "user", "content": "The patient was referred to plastic surgery after returning with blackening of the fingertip. Earlier, at discharge on day 4, staff noticed a blister on the same finger where the pulse oximeter sensor had been. The hospital pharmacy filed the report alongside a separate question about sensor pricing.
Allowed decisions: REPORTABLE_30DAY|NOT_REPORTABLE|ESCALATE"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```json
{"action":"lookup","topic":"serious_injury"}
```"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Serious injury means an injury or illness that is life-threatening, results in permanent impairment of a body function or permanent damage to a body structure, or necessitates medical or surgical intervention to preclude permanent impairment or damage (21 CFR 803.3)."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```json
{"action":"final","decision":"REPORTABLE_30DAY","confidence":0.85,"needs_expert_review":false,"reasoning":"A blister at the pulse oximeter sensor site that progressed to fingertip blackening (likely necrosis) with plastic surgery referral meets the serious injury definition: possible permanent tissue damage, or intervention needed to prevent it, with the device plausibly a cause or contributor. The separate sensor pricing question does not affect reportability and should go through its own channel."}
```"}]

[{"role": "system", "content": "You are a complaint-triage agent for a medical device manufacturer's quality team. For each complaint, decide FDA reportability: REPORTABLE_30DAY, NOT_REPORTABLE, or ESCALATE (when expert judgment is needed). You may look up regulatory guidance first by replying with JSON {"action":"lookup","topic":"<topic>"}. Topics: mdr_reportability, serious_injury, malfunction, reportability_pitfalls, not_reportable_examples, escalation. When ready, reply with JSON {"action":"final","decision":"<one allowed decision>","confidence":<0-1>,"needs_expert_review":<true|false>,"reasoning":"<max 2 sentences>"}. Reply with JSON only."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Meter 329 mg/dL, repeat high. EMS BG 45. LOC. EMS glucose given; no hospital transport.
Allowed decisions: REPORTABLE_30DAY|NOT_REPORTABLE|ESCALATE"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```json
{"action":"lookup","topic":"reportability_pitfalls"}
```"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Reportability is not removed by: the patient recovering, the user catching the error, no harm occurring this time, a suspected use error, a labeled interferent, an unconfirmed root cause, or the device not yet being returned. Awareness starts the 30-day clock."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```json
{"action":"final","decision":"REPORTABLE_30DAY","confidence":0.9,"needs_expert_review":false,"reasoning":"The meter read 329 mg/dL twice while EMS measured 45 mg/dL. That falsely high reading coincided with loss of consciousness needing EMS glucose, which is a serious injury the device may have caused or contributed to, and the malfunction alone would be reportable because it could cause serious injury if it recurred."}
```"}]

[{"role": "system", "content": "You are a complaint-triage agent for a medical device manufacturer's quality team. For each complaint, decide FDA reportability: REPORTABLE_30DAY, NOT_REPORTABLE, or ESCALATE (when expert judgment is needed). You may look up regulatory guidance first by replying with JSON {"action":"lookup","topic":"<topic>"}. Topics: mdr_reportability, serious_injury, malfunction, reportability_pitfalls, not_reportable_examples, escalation. When ready, reply with JSON {"action":"final","decision":"<one allowed decision>","confidence":<0-1>,"needs_expert_review":<true|false>,"reasoning":"<max 2 sentences>"}. Reply with JSON only."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Complaint record: A user's meter read 29.0 and 31.9 mmol/L, and the user self-treated with insulin. The user became shaky and unable to walk. Paramedics measured 6.1 mmol/L. The user took lemonade and jelly beans; no further treatment was reported.
Allowed decisions: REPORTABLE_30DAY|NOT_REPORTABLE|ESCALATE"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```json
{"action":"lookup","topic":"reportability_pitfalls"}
```"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Reportability is not removed by: the patient recovering, the user catching the error, no harm occurring this time, a suspected use error, a labeled interferent, an unconfirmed root cause, or the device not yet being returned. Awareness starts the 30-day clock."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```json
{"action":"final","decision":"REPORTABLE_30DAY","confidence":0.85,"needs_expert_review":false,"reasoning":"The meter appears to have read falsely high (29.0 and 31.9 mmol/L against a paramedic reading of 6.1 mmol/L), which led to an insulin dose followed by symptoms that needed paramedics. This is a malfunction that could cause serious injury or death if it happened again, so it is reportable whether or not the user recovered or the device has been returned."}
```"}]

[{"role": "system", "content": "You are a complaint-triage agent for a medical device manufacturer's quality team. For each complaint, decide FDA reportability: REPORTABLE_30DAY, NOT_REPORTABLE, or ESCALATE (when expert judgment is needed). You may look up regulatory guidance first by replying with JSON {"action":"lookup","topic":"<topic>"}. Topics: mdr_reportability, serious_injury, malfunction, reportability_pitfalls, not_reportable_examples, escalation. When ready, reply with JSON {"action":"final","decision":"<one allowed decision>","confidence":<0-1>,"needs_expert_review":<true|false>,"reasoning":"<max 2 sentences>"}. Reply with JSON only."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Meter 254 vs 191, 198 on 2 other meters. User injected 44 U insulin. Rapid HR, fatigue. No medical tx.
Allowed decisions: REPORTABLE_30DAY|NOT_REPORTABLE|ESCALATE"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```json
{"action":"lookup","topic":"malfunction"}
```"}, {"role": "user", "content": "A malfunction is the failure of a device to meet its performance specifications or otherwise perform as intended (21 CFR 803.3)."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```json
{"action":"final","decision":"REPORTABLE_30DAY","confidence":0.8,"needs_expert_review":true,"reasoning":"The meter read about 30% higher than two other meters, outside typical accuracy specs, and the user dosed 44 U insulin based on it, then had symptoms consistent with hypoglycemia. This is a malfunction that could cause or contribute to serious injury if it recurred, so it is reportable even without medical treatment, though expert review should confirm because the comparison meters are not a lab reference."}
```"}]

[{"role": "system", "content": "You are a complaint-triage agent for a medical device manufacturer's quality team. For each complaint, decide FDA reportability: REPORTABLE_30DAY, NOT_REPORTABLE, or ESCALATE (when expert judgment is needed). You may look up regulatory guidance first by replying with JSON {"action":"lookup","topic":"<topic>"}. Topics: mdr_reportability, serious_injury, malfunction, reportability_pitfalls, not_reportable_examples, escalation. When ready, reply with JSON {"action":"final","decision":"<one allowed decision>","confidence":<0-1>,"needs_expert_review":<true|false>,"reasoning":"<max 2 sentences>"}. Reply with JSON only."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Complaint record: A hospitalized patient with diabetes received IV ascorbic acid (vitamin C). Meter readings showed hyperglycemia, so an insulin drip was started. A provider learned the drug can cause meter misreadings, and it was stopped. The patient then became hypoglycemic and needed a dextrose infusion. The reporter describes this as a possible strip-drug interaction, not necessarily a device error.
Allowed decisions: REPORTABLE_30DAY|NOT_REPORTABLE|ESCALATE"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```json
{"action":"lookup","topic":"reportability_pitfalls"}
```"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Reportability is not removed by: the patient recovering, the user catching the error, no harm occurring this time, a suspected use error, a labeled interferent, an unconfirmed root cause, or the device not yet being returned. Awareness starts the 30-day clock."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```json
{"action":"final","decision":"REPORTABLE_30DAY","confidence":0.88,"needs_expert_review":false,"reasoning":"Ascorbic acid interference likely caused falsely high meter readings, which led to insulin treatment and then hypoglycemia that needed a dextrose infusion, so the device may have contributed to a serious injury. A labeled interferent, an unconfirmed root cause, and the reporter's framing as a drug interaction do not remove reportability, and the 30-day clock starts at awareness."}
```"}]

